\description{
Use \code{lv_axis()} in a \code{trend_formula} in any position that takes a
factor variable, to give each latent factor its own effect. In a
model with no latent factors it gives each series its own effect.
Use it when a covariate should act differently on different series,
such as temperature raising one species and lowering another.
}
\details{
For example:
\preformatted{
trend_formula = ~ s(elev, by = lv_axis()) + AR(n_lv = 2)
trend_formula = ~ temp:lv_axis() + AR(n_lv = 2)
trend_formula = ~ (1 + temp | lv_axis()) + AR()
trend_formula = ~ s(temp, lv_axis(), bs = "fs") + AR()
}

With \code{n_lv} set on the trend constructor, each factor
\verb{k = 1, ..., n_lv} takes its own effect. The effect of a covariate
on series \code{s} is the sum over factors of the loading \code{Z[s, k]} times
the effect on factor \code{k}. This is constrained ordination, the model
\code{\link[=jsdgam]{jsdgam()}} fits. Every term of such a \code{trend_formula} is then
evaluated once per factor and time. Each covariate must take one
value per time.

With no \code{n_lv}, each series takes its own effect on the latent
state.

The series column itself is not supported in a \code{trend_formula}
term. Write \code{lv_axis()} for an effect that differs along the
trend's axis. An effect of the series on the observations belongs
in \code{formula}. \code{by = trend} is deprecated and is treated as
\code{by = lv_axis()}.
}
\section{What each form estimates}{

With no latent factors, \code{lv_axis()} is each series:
\itemize{
\item \code{~ x:lv_axis()} gives a slope of \code{x} per series.
\item \code{~ lv_axis()} gives a level per series.
\item \code{~ (1 + x | lv_axis())} gives levels and slopes that are
partially pooled across series.
\item \code{~ s(x, by = lv_axis())} gives a smooth of \code{x} per series,
and \code{~ s(x, lv_axis(), bs = "fs")} gives smooths that share a
smoothness penalty.
}
With \code{n_lv} latent factors, each of these is estimated once per
factor. The coefficient of one factor has no scale of its own. The
loadings and the factor effects trade scale. The data identify
their product. \code{\link[=conditional_effects.mvgam]{conditional_effects.mvgam()}} and \code{\link[=predict.mvgam]{predict.mvgam()}}
report that product, the effect of the covariate on each series.
\code{\link[=conditional_effects.mvgam]{conditional_effects.mvgam()}} draws a panel per series. When every
\code{lv_axis()} term is a slope or a level, \code{\link[=summary.mvgam]{summary.mvgam()}} reports the
per-factor coefficients in the basis of the identified loadings
\code{Z_tilde}.
}

\examples{
# `lv_axis()` marks a term in a formula. Called by itself it
# returns NULL.
lv_axis()

\dontrun{
# Three series whose seasonal effect differs: 1, 0.5 and -1
set.seed(1)
dat <- sim_mvgam(family = gaussian(), n_series = 3L,
                 n_timepoints = 60L)$data_train
dat$season <- sin(2 * pi * dat$time / 12)
dat$y <- dat$y + c(1, 0.5, -1)[as.integer(dat$series)] * dat$season

# One seasonal slope per series
mod <- mvgam(
  formula       = y ~ 1,
  trend_formula = ~ season:lv_axis() + AR(p = 1),
  data          = dat,
  family        = gaussian(),
  chains        = 2,
  silent        = 2
)
summary(mod)
conditional_effects(mod)

# With two latent factors, each factor takes a seasonal slope. The
# loadings carry the two slopes to the three series. The prior
# table lists one coefficient per factor. A smooth takes the same
# marker: `s(season, by = lv_axis())`
get_prior(
  mvgam_formula(y ~ 1,
                trend_formula = ~ season:lv_axis() + AR(p = 1, n_lv = 2)),
  data   = dat,
  family = gaussian()
)
}

